In this lesson we'll look at how to turn fractions into decimals, and how to tell straight away whether the decimal will stop or keep going forever. So here's the big idea — any fraction can become a decimal just by dividing the top number by the bottom number. You can do that with long division, just remember to bring down a decimal point and add zeros whenever you need to. Now, the clever shortcut is this: once your fraction is in its simplest form, look at the bottom number. If its only prime factors are twos and fives, then the decimal will terminate, meaning it stops neatly. For example, three quarters ends in point seven five, and seven twentieths ends in point three five, because their denominators only have twos and fives in them. But if the denominator has any other prime factor, like a three or a six or a seven, the decimal will not terminate — it will keep repeating the same block of digits forever. We call that a recurring decimal, and we mark the repeating block with a little bar on top. So before you even divide, just peek at the bottom of the fraction and you'll know what's coming. Now let's see it in action.